+    /// A copy of the secrets page carrying NO message key at all, for a guest plane that must not be able to
+    /// speak to the PSP on its own behalf.
+    ///
+    /// `copy_for_vmpl` clears only `0..vmpl`, so a guest at VMPL2 keeps VMPCK2 and VMPCK3 - and with them it
+    /// can request a signed report over its own GHCB, with `report_data` of its choosing and any VMPL from its
+    /// own level downwards, without involving this SVSM at all. Every gate the SVSM puts in front of a report
+    /// is then bypassed by not asking for one: an app-naming protocol that admits artifacts before it will
+    /// name a plane is worth nothing while the plane can name itself.
+    ///
+    /// Clearing all four makes the SVSM the only holder of a message key in this guest, so the SVSM's
+    /// protocols become the only path to a report. Two consequences, both intended:
+    ///
+    ///   * Linux's `sev-guest` driver finds no usable VMPCK and fails to probe, so configfs-tsm offers no
+    ///     report interface inside the guest. Anything that used it must move to an SVSM protocol.
+    ///   * that failure is itself the key-absence evidence this project wanted: a guest that can load
+    ///     `sev-guest` with `vmpck_id=0` demonstrably holds VMPCK0 and is therefore NOT beneath a VMPL0
+    ///     monitor, which is the test the forgeable `vmpl0=refused` tuple could never perform
+    ///     (isolation/DESIGN.md section 12).
+    pub fn copy_with_no_vmpck(&self) -> Box<SecretsPage> {
+        let mut sp = self.copy_for_vmpl(0);
+        for idx in 0..VMPL_MAX {
+            sp.clear_vmpck(idx);
+        }
+        sp
+    }
